# agent-compose deployment configuration
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # edit .env
#   docker compose up -d
#   # To also start the web UI:
#   # docker compose --profile with-ui up -d

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Published services
# The installer manages release image references internally. Docker registry
# mirrors are configured in dockerd and do not change those references.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port published by the web UI and reverse proxy when the with-ui profile
# is enabled.
AGENT_COMPOSE_HTTP_PORT=80
# Set to with-ui to start the web frontend alongside the daemon. The installer
# writes this from its "install web UI" choice; editing it here changes what a
# plain `docker compose up -d` brings up.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=with-ui
# Host directory bind-mounted at /data. The installer persists the legacy
# ./data/agent-compose layout when upgrading an installation that uses it.
AGENT_COMPOSE_DATA_DIR=./data
# Separate host directory for the UI server database and UI-side state. Use an
# absolute path when the UI data should live outside the daemon deployment dir.
# The UI also reads <AGENT_COMPOSE_DATA_DIR>/sandboxes through a separate
# read-only mount so it can display Codex and Claude agent records.
AGENT_COMPOSE_UI_DATA_DIR=./data/agent-compose-ui

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# These settings are consumed by the agent-compose-ui server when the with-ui
# profile is enabled. The daemon itself does not use browser cookie/OAuth auth.
# AUTH_SECRET must be stable and high entropy so login sessions survive restarts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AUTH_USERNAME=admin
AUTH_PASSWORD=
AUTH_SECRET=
AUTH_SESSION_TTL=24h

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Daemon network and storage
# These are daemon-level settings. The published Docker image already provides
# the same container defaults, but keeping them here makes remote deployments
# explicit and easy to audit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HTTP_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:7410
# This listener is also the address a sandbox uses for the engine's outbound
# forward proxy, which enforces a declared policy.network on every driver whose
# sandbox can reach it. Keep it reachable from the sandbox network: the engine
# does not verify reachability, so an unreachable listener silently drops
# enforcement instead of reporting the declaration unenforced.
# Optional shared bearer token for daemon HTTP(S) control-plane requests.
# Leave empty to disable daemon authentication. Unix socket access, health,
# runtime LLM facade, Jupyter proxy, and webhook ingestion use separate trust
# boundaries and do not require this token.
AGENT_COMPOSE_AUTH_TOKEN=
DATA_ROOT=/data
# SANDBOX_ROOT=/data/sandboxes
# Maximum runtime connections to the shared SQLite database. Startup migrations
# always use one connection, and in-memory databases always remain single-connection.
# SQLITE_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=4

# Jupyter proxy base path. Keep this in sync with any external reverse proxy.
JUPYTER_PROXY_BASE=/jupyter

# Guest-reachable daemon URL used by runtime LLM facade config.
# Docker Compose defaults this to http://agent-compose:7410.
# AGENT_COMPOSE_RUNTIME_BASE_URL=

# Docker sandbox network placement. The docker-compose.sandbox-network.yml
# overlay declares the networks and dual-homes the daemon onto them, so enable
# it by setting, in .env:
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.sandbox-network.yml
#
# By default newly-created sandboxes join the placement-only
# `agent-compose-sandbox` network; a sandbox on it still uses the host's real
# NIC. The strict outer default-deny profile instead joins each sandbox only to
# `agent-compose-sandbox-strict`, a user-defined internal network with no route
# off it. Every process in the container, including one Docker starts outside
# sandbox init (for example `docker exec`), is then denied off-network egress.
# The switch is only read when SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS=enforce; audit and off keep
# today's placement. Select the strict profile with:
#   SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS=enforce
#   DOCKER_SANDBOX_NETWORK=agent-compose-sandbox-strict
#   DOCKER_SANDBOX_NETWORK_STRICT=true
#
# In strict mode DOCKER_SANDBOX_NETWORK must name an existing user-defined
# Docker network created with --internal, and a containerized daemon must be
# attached to it; otherwise each sandbox start fails closed instead of falling
# back to the daemon or default network. AGENT_COMPOSE_RUNTIME_BASE_URL must
# name the daemon on that network (its alias on the internal network, or its
# gateway address); the overlay keeps the `agent-compose` alias, so the Compose
# default http://agent-compose:7410 works. The layer admits the daemon
# endpoints on that network, the host bridge gateway, and other containers on
# the network, and denies everything outside it; Docker's embedded DNS resolver
# still answers queries. Sandboxes created before strict mode was enabled keep
# their old network and must be removed and created again.
# DOCKER_SANDBOX_NETWORK=agent-compose-sandbox-strict
# DOCKER_SANDBOX_NETWORK_STRICT=true
#
# Outside strict mode the daemon injects the forward proxy whenever that URL is
# a non-loopback address but does not verify it is reachable, so a misconfigured
# network silently drops enforcement.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime selection
# The default driver is docker. DEFAULT_IMAGE is the guest image used for new
# sandboxes unless a more specific driver image is configured.
# The base docker-compose.yml is Docker-only and does not require KVM.
# BoxLite and Microsandbox require the KVM overlay; the installer detects KVM
# and persists its Compose selection. For a manual KVM-enabled deployment, use:
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.kvm.yml
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUNTIME_DRIVER=docker
# Experimental: make newly-created sandboxes run the agent-compose init process
# as PID 1. The guest image must contain the binary at SANDBOX_INIT_BINARY (the
# guest image built by this change does). The process model is persisted with
# each sandbox, so changing the flag does not change an existing (including a
# stopped) sandbox when it is resumed.
# SANDBOX_INIT_ENABLED=false
# SANDBOX_INIT_BINARY=/.agent-compose/ac-init
# Host runtime directory for one <sandbox-id>/init.sock per Docker/BoxLite
# sandbox. Kubernetes and Microsandbox carry the protocol through an
# `ac-init --client` guest exec instead. Defaults to
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-compose or /run/agent-compose. The Compose deployment
# uses a DATA_ROOT subdirectory because the daemon's own /run is not the Docker
# Engine host's /run.
# SANDBOX_INIT_SOCKET_DIR=/data/run/agent-compose
# Experimental: control workload egress from the init process. off (the default)
# changes nothing; audit reports every connect without changing its outcome;
# enforce redirects every TCP connect through the daemon egress proxy, which
# requires the proxy credential the daemon installs for a declared network
# policy. The guest image must contain the egress-aware ac-init and the guest
# kernel must support seccomp user notification (>= 5.5). Docker/Kubernetes
# init containers receive CAP_SYS_PTRACE for pidfd_getfd; the workload does not
# inherit it. DNS queries are redirected to a sandbox-local policy resolver that
# answers synthetic 198.18.0.0/15 addresses (AAAA empty) and passes engine-owned
# hostnames through to their real addresses.
# SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS=off

# Experimental Docker-only independent supervisor. With enforce, the workload
# container is created with NetworkMode=none (no external NIC) and reaches the
# daemon egress proxy only through a per-sandbox ac-supervisor process and a
# host-mounted Unix domain socket. The workload cannot reach the daemon proxy
# directly, so a process Docker starts outside sandbox init (for example
# docker exec) is also default-denied; the init relay authenticates to the
# supervisor with a per-sandbox random token delivered over the authenticated
# init control socket, so it is never part of the container environment (a
# docker exec process cannot read it). Requires SANDBOX_INIT_ENABLED=true and
# SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS=enforce. The daemon image ships the binary at
# /app/ac-supervisor. Jupyter cannot be exposed while this mode is on, because
# Docker cannot publish a port on a container with no interface; a sandbox that
# requests Jupyter is refused instead of silently losing it. Ordinary egress is
# still decided by the daemon forward proxy; only the engine-owned LLM facade
# and telemetry endpoints are connected directly by the supervisor.
# SANDBOX_SUPERVISOR=off
# SANDBOX_SUPERVISOR_BINARY=/app/ac-supervisor
# Optional numeric uid/gid for the supervisor process. The workload can stay
# root or use SANDBOX_INIT_WORKLOAD_UID; the supervisor only needs write access
# to its socket directory while starting, which the daemon grants and then
# restores before the workload starts. Both must be set together.
# SANDBOX_SUPERVISOR_UID=1000
# SANDBOX_SUPERVISOR_GID=1000

# Experimental: confine workload filesystem access with Landlock. off (the
# default) changes nothing. enforce makes every command init starts run under a
# ruleset that allows reads/execution of system paths, read-write access to
# workspace/home/state/runtime/logs/tmp, and denies everything else, including
# /.agent-compose and the init control directory. It does not affect network
# mediation. The guest kernel must provide Landlock (>= 5.13; ABI >= 1).
# SANDBOX_INIT_LANDLOCK=off
# What to do when Landlock is requested but the guest kernel cannot provide it.
# fail (the default) refuses to start the sandbox rather than report isolation
# it does not have; warn starts the sandbox without filesystem confinement and
# logs the degradation. With fail, the daemon also requires the init handshake
# to advertise the landlock-enforce capability.
# SANDBOX_INIT_LANDLOCK_FALLBACK=fail
# Optional: run workload processes as this non-root numeric uid/gid instead of
# root. The init process stays root so it can mediate sockets and keep the
# control socket; it chowns /workspace, /root, and /data/{state,runtime,logs}
# before exec and drops the exec child to the identity. Both variables must be
# set together; 0/0 (the default) keeps the historical root workload.
# SANDBOX_INIT_WORKLOAD_UID=1000
# SANDBOX_INIT_WORKLOAD_GID=1000

# Opt-in guest-side JSONL audit sink for connections observed by ac-init. When
# true, the daemon injects a path under GUEST_LOG_ROOT (default /data/logs) and
# init appends one event per observed connect. Docker and Kubernetes already
# capture init stderr, so this is off by default and is intended for drivers
# whose guest stderr is not collected (for example Microsandbox). Requires
# SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS=audit or enforce.
# SANDBOX_INIT_EGRESS_AUDIT_LOG=false

# What to do when a project declares an outbound network policy (policy.network
# with default: deny-all) that the selected runtime driver cannot apply. warn
# (the default) runs the sandbox and reports the declaration as unenforced;
# refuse fails the sandbox instead of running it with wider egress than declared.
# EGRESS_UNENFORCEABLE_POLICY=warn
# Optional append-only JSONL audit log for forward-proxy decisions. One line is
# one allow/deny with the sandbox, destination, rule, generation, and the
# sandbox-init session/marker identity. Empty disables persistence. The file is
# created 0600 under the data mount, for example:
# EGRESS_AUDIT_LOG=/data/work/egress-audit.jsonl
# Optional daemon-wide outbound network policy. The file has the same
# default/allow/deny body as an agent's policy.network block and applies to every
# sandbox as an outer bound; agents may narrow it but cannot widen it. It is read
# once at startup, so changing it requires a daemon restart. Keep it under the
# mounted data directory when running the Compose deployment.
# EGRESS_GLOBAL_POLICY_FILE=/data/work/global-network-policy.yaml
# Optional L7 HTTPS enforcement. Disabled by default: an allowance that declares
# methods or paths on an HTTPS endpoint then fails closed instead of being
# forwarded uninspected. Enable to terminate TLS with a per-host leaf signed by
# the CA below, apply method/path rules, and audit each HTTP request.
# EGRESS_L7_TLS_MITM=true
# The CA the proxy signs leaves with. Set either the two file variables or the two
# inline PEM variables, never both. If neither is set an ephemeral CA is generated
# at daemon start, which a sandbox cannot trust across a restart. Generate one with
# openssl and keep the key out of the repository.
# EGRESS_L7_CA_CERT_FILE=/data/work/egress-ca.pem
# EGRESS_L7_CA_KEY_FILE=/data/work/egress-ca-key.pem
# EGRESS_L7_CA_CERT_PEM=...
# EGRESS_L7_CA_KEY_PEM=...
# Absolute guest path of the CA bundle. The file itself must be installed by the
# guest image or a mounted volume; setting this injects SSL_CERT_FILE,
# CURL_CA_BUNDLE, REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS, and GIT_SSL_CAINFO.
# EGRESS_L7_CA_BUNDLE_GUEST_PATH=/etc/agent-compose/egress-ca.pem
DEFAULT_IMAGE=chaitin/agent-compose-guest:latest
# Sandbox-host image for KVM deployments (BoxLite/Microsandbox). The installer
# manages this from the release manifest and advances it independently of the
# daemon image, so a daemon-only upgrade does not recreate the sandbox-host.
# It never falls back to AGENT_COMPOSE_IMAGE; when unset it uses the release tag
# (chaitin/agent-compose:latest) directly.
# AGENT_COMPOSE_SANDBOX_HOST_IMAGE=
# How the daemon obtains the sandbox-host. external (the default) only attaches
# to a host managed outside the daemon (Docker Compose); the daemon never spawns
# one, so an unprivileged host can never be created inside the daemon container.
# managed lets the daemon spawn the host on first use, for local development and
# native deployments only; it does not guarantee host survival across upgrades.
# AGENT_COMPOSE_SANDBOX_HOST_MODE=external
# Optional path to the guest ABI manifest the guest image publishes
# (guest-images/guest-abi.json in this repository), mounted into the daemon.
# When set, the engine capability API reports the provider versions and
# capabilities from that manifest instead of the daemon's compiled table. When
# unset, unreadable, or describing a different guest ABI, the daemon logs a
# warning and keeps the compiled table, so existing deployments are unaffected.
# GUEST_ABI_MANIFEST=/data/work/guest-abi.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime internals (advanced)
# Change these only when your deployment environment requires custom runtime
# paths, image cache behavior, or driver-specific images.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Daemon process timezone. By default the Compose deployment inherits the
# Linux host's /etc/localtime. Set TZ only to override the host timezone.
# TZ=Asia/Shanghai

# Guest paths and ports.
# --------------------------------------
# GUEST_WORKSPACE=/workspace
# GUEST_STATE_ROOT=/data/state
# GUEST_RUNTIME_ROOT=/data/runtime
# GUEST_LOG_ROOT=/data/logs
# JUPYTER_GUEST_PORT=8888

# Driver artifact paths.
# --------------------------------------
# BOXLITE_RUNTIME_DIR=/app/boxlite/runtime
# MICROSANDBOX_HOME=/data/microsandbox
# MICROSANDBOX_MSB_PATH=/app/microsandbox/bin/msb
# MICROSANDBOX_LIB_PATH=/app/microsandbox/lib/libmicrosandbox_go_ffi.so
# Usually this is auto-detected from the daemon container's /data mount.
# DOCKER_HOST_SANDBOX_ROOT=/absolute/host/path/to/data/sandboxes

# Driver-specific images.
# --------------------------------------
# DOCKER_DEFAULT_IMAGE=debian:bookworm-slim
# MICROSANDBOX_DEFAULT_IMAGE=debian:bookworm-slim

# Microsandbox in-VM DNS proxy. It refuses answers that point at private
# addresses, which is what stops DNS rebinding, and is on by default. Set it to
# false only when guests must resolve internal names (an intranet service on a
# 10/8 address, for example): the switch is global to the deployment and has no
# per-domain exemption, so every sandbox in it can then be steered at internal
# addresses by a name it resolves.
# MICROSANDBOX_DNS_REBIND_PROTECTION=true

# Image registry and cache.
# --------------------------------------
# IMAGE_REGISTRY=docker.io
# IMAGE_STORE_MODE=auto
# IMAGE_CACHE_ROOT=/data/images
# IMAGE_INSECURE_REGISTRIES=
# Shared cache expiration threshold. Defaults to 168h; 0 disables expiration
# classification. Cache deletion is always explicit through cache prune/rm.
# CACHE_TTL=168h

# Automatic retention cleanup. Each TTL is also its feature switch: 0 disables
# that policy. Workspace cleanup only reclaims workspace data. Sandbox retention
# reclaims workspace when needed, archives the remaining audit data, and then
# removes the original sandbox resources. Cleanup does not use disk watermarks.
# CLEANUP_INTERVAL=1h
# WORKSPACE_CLEANUP_TTL=0
# SANDBOX_RETENTION_TTL=0
# SANDBOX_ARCHIVE_ROOT=/data/archives/sandboxes
# IMAGE_CACHE_CLEANUP_TTL=0

# BoxLite and Microsandbox microVM resources.
# --------------------------------------
# BOXLITE_HOME=/data/boxlite
# BOX_ROOTFS_PATH=
# SANDBOX_CPUS=4
# Unified BoxLite/Microsandbox default; override for larger workloads.
# SANDBOX_MEMORY_MIB=4096
# For Microsandbox, SANDBOX_DISK_SIZE_GB is the qcow2/ext4 root-disk capacity
# shared by system files and /var/lib/docker, and the bind-mount write quota.
# SANDBOX_DISK_SIZE_GB=6

# Docker and MicroSandbox storage.
# --------------------------------------
# DOCKER_HOME=/data/docker
# MICROSANDBOX_INSECURE_REGISTRIES=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM (optional). Configure if schedulers or agents call an LLM.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM_API_ENDPOINT=https://api.openai.com
# LLM_API_PROTOCOL=responses
# LLM_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=
# Static extra HTTP headers as a JSON object. Authorization is still
# generated from LLM_API_KEY and cannot be overridden here.
# The daemon consumes this value without exposing it to guest runtimes. It is
# shared by env-backed OpenAI and Anthropic providers, so every configured
# header must be safe to send to each configured upstream.
# LLM_API_HEADERS={"Bizscenario":"mobile-learning","Branchid":"010801"}
# Maximum output tokens the runtime LLM facade injects into each proxied
# upstream request. codex does not send max_output_tokens itself, and the
# protocol bridge defaults max_completion_tokens to a small value (e.g. 4096),
# so proxies that honor that field silently truncate long agent outputs.
# Set this for models served via OpenAI-compatible gateways (DeepSeek, vLLM,
# Ollama, third-party proxies) to stop mid-response truncation.
# LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=65536
# Bounds direct LLM calls and the facade's wait for upstream response headers.
# Established streaming responses do not have a total duration limit.
# LLM_TIMEOUT=60s
# Codex retries 5xx and transport failures separately from reconnecting a
# dropped stream. Set either retry limit to 0 to disable that retry layer.
# CODEX_REQUEST_MAX_RETRIES=1
# CODEX_STREAM_MAX_RETRIES=1
# Defaults to LLM_TIMEOUT when unset.
# CODEX_STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT=60s
# At startup, and after each LLM provider write, the daemon checks what each
# enabled upstream actually serves: it calls the connection's /v1/models and
# sends one minimal ping per protocol in the connection's family. The check is a
# diagnostic; it never changes routing, and its results are logged, not stored.
# It is ON by default, so an upgrade adds that outbound traffic to every enabled
# upstream; set false to keep the daemon from issuing any probe request.
# LLM_UPSTREAM_PROBE=true
# Bounds each probe request and the whole startup sweep.
# LLM_PROBE_TIMEOUT=15s

# OpenAI-compatible chat completions.
# --------------------------------------
# LLM_API_PROTOCOL=chat_completions

# Anthropic-family facade bootstrap.
# --------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
# ANTHROPIC_API_ENDPOINT=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=
# CLAUDE_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OAuth (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OAUTH_APIKEY=
# OAUTH_SECRET=
# OAUTH_BASE_URL=
# OAUTH_CALLBACK_URL=
# OAUTH_SCOPES=profile
# OAUTH_CLIENT_AUTH_METHOD=client_secret_post

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operational tuning (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AGENT_TIMEOUT=10h
# SCHEDULER_RUN_TIMEOUT=20m
# Which Projects receive an event a Project publishes: project (default) keeps
# it in the publisher's Project; daemon delivers it to every Project.
# EVENT_DELIVERY_SCOPE=project
# Deprecated compatibility fallback: LOADER_RUN_TIMEOUT=20m
# SANDBOX_START_TIMEOUT=30m
# SANDBOX_STOP_TIMEOUT=30s
# Grace period used by explicit graceful sandbox stops when the request does
# not provide --grace-period. Must be a positive Go duration; default: 10s.
# SANDBOX_GRACEFUL_STOP_TIMEOUT=10s
# Max time to wait for Jupyter to become ready inside a guest before failing the
# sandbox start. Defaults to 120s to allow for guest cold starts; proceeds as
# soon as Jupyter is ready. Non-positive or unparseable values use the default.
# JUPYTER_READY_TIMEOUT=120s
# WEBHOOK_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=1048576
# WEBHOOK_QUEUE_DEFAULT_WORKERS=8
# WEBHOOK_QUEUE_RULES_JSON=[{"name":"repo-agent-compose","workers":2,"match":{"topic":"webhook.github.push","payload":{"body.repository.full_name":"chaitin/agent-compose"}}}]
# WORKSPACE_UPLOAD_LIMIT_BYTES=1073741824

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deprecated environment aliases
# SESSION_ROOT, DOCKER_HOST_SESSION_ROOT, SESSION_START_TIMEOUT, and
# SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT remain accepted as aliases. Use the SANDBOX_*
# replacements above; they take precedence when both names are set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Native agent telemetry (optional; sent directly from guests, not the daemon).
# Use an OTLP/HTTP base URL reachable from every guest. No /v1/logs suffix.
# AGENT_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://collector.example:4318
# Comma-separated name=percent-encoded-value pairs; leave credentials empty here.
# AGENT_TELEMETRY_OTLP_HEADERS=
# Explicit opt-in for prompt/tool content, OpenCode LLM spans and DSH FULL logs.
# AGENT_TELEMETRY_CAPTURE_CONTENT=false
